-----------------------------------------------------------------
(sed  supports regex)

eg : lets suppose we have to modify bindu in bindu:/bin/bash  to buddy:/bin/bash

 we can do :
	cat /etc/passwd | grep bindu | cut -d : -f 1,7 | tr bindu buddy
	
	it will change bindu:bin/bash  to  buddy:/byd/bash
	-> its bcz tr performs character to characrer replacement
	So, b->b i->u n->d d->d u->y  so bin will also be changed into byd (i->u->y && n->d) 

 	so in this case sed comes handy 
	we can change bindu:/bin/bash easily by using the command:
	cat /etc/passwd | grep bindu | cut -d : -f 1,7 | sed 's/bindu/buddy/'

	
	Note: It is a change in the output printed in the screen and not a system change.


 
